--TEST--
`deadline` bounds the whole call and not each child, `all` and `map` agree on that, and `limit` is the other half of the same bag — `rule:concurrency/limit-and-deadline-are-the-only-bounds` and `rule:concurrency/nothing-is-still-running-when-a-call-returns`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:concurrency/limit-and-deadline
// The neighbouring case pins that `timeout` and `race` do not exist as names.
// This is the other half: what the surviving spelling actually means at run
// time. Five children of 60ms under a 200ms deadline is the discriminator —
// run at once they finish inside it, run one at a time they cannot, and the
// only thing that changed between the two calls below is `limit`. A `deadline`
// that were per child would return from both.

array<int> $work = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

try {
    Core\Task::map($work, fn (int $n): int => {
        Core\Time::sleep(60ms);
        return $n;
    }, {deadline: 200ms});
    echo "map returned", "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $e) {
    echo "map hit the deadline", "\n";
}

// Same children, same deadline, serialized by `limit`. § 3: a child past the
// limit is scheduled rather than refused, so this is five waits end to end and
// not four refusals.
try {
    Core\Task::map($work, fn (int $n): int => {
        Core\Time::sleep(60ms);
        return $n;
    }, {limit: 1, deadline: 200ms});
    echo "map returned", "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $e) {
    echo "map hit the deadline", "\n";
}

// The agreement half: one options bag, two members, and the answer must not
// depend on which member was asked (`docs/agent/conventions.md`'s agreement
// shape). A `Core\Task::all` that grew its own reading of `deadline` fails
// here while still looking right on the `map` lines above.
try {
    Core\Task::all({
        a: fn(): int => {
            Core\Time::sleep(60ms);
            return 1;
        },
        b: fn(): int => {
            Core\Time::sleep(60ms);
            return 2;
        },
        c: fn(): int => {
            Core\Time::sleep(60ms);
            return 3;
        },
        d: fn(): int => {
            Core\Time::sleep(60ms);
            return 4;
        },
        e: fn(): int => {
            Core\Time::sleep(60ms);
            return 5;
        },
    }, {limit: 1, deadline: 200ms});
    echo "all returned", "\n";
} catch (TimeoutError $e) {
    echo "all hit the deadline", "\n";
}

// `limit`'s own boundary, in the same bag: zero admits no child, so the group
// could never finish and saying so is a `LogicError` rather than a wait that
// ends at the deadline (`Core\Task`'s reference card, `rule:core-api/reference-card`).
try {
    Core\Task::map($work, fn (int $n): int => $n, {limit: 0});
    echo "a limit of zero ran", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $zero) {
    echo "a limit of zero is refused", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
map returned
map hit the deadline
all hit the deadline
a limit of zero is refused
